n-names></name><name><surname>Narayan</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Augmentation of the amplitude of electrocardiographic QRS complexes immediately following hemodialysis: A study of 26 hemodialysis sessions of a single patient, aided by measurements of resistance/reactance/impedance</article-title><source>J Electrocardiol</source><year>2003</year><volume>36</volume><fpage>263</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12942491</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="R31"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Madias</surname><given-names>JE</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Significance of shortening of the mean QRS duration of the standard electrocardiogram in patients developing peripheral edema</article-title><source>Amer J Cardiol</source><year>2002</year><volume>89</volume><fpage>1444</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12062748</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="R32"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Madias</surname><given-names>JE</given-names></name><name><surname>Macfarlane</surname><given-names>PW</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Artificial attenuation of ECG voltage produces shorteningof the corresponding QRS duration: Clinical implications for patients with edema</article-title><source>Pacing Clin   Electrophysiol</source><year>2005</year><volume>28</volume><fpage>1060</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16221264</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>P-wave and QRS complex from leads II and aVL before and after HD; note the increase in the amplitude of QRS complexes in both leads following HD, and a slight increase in the amplitude of P-wave in lead II. PWdu did not change in lead II, while PWdu in aVL was not measurable either before or after HD, since the P-wave could not be differentiated from "noise".</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ipej070085-01"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Echocardiogram (4-chamber view) of a patient before (left panel) and after (right panel) HD.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ipej070085-02"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Standard 12-lead ECG (single standardization, and speed 25 mm/s) revealing post-HD augmentation of SQRS; P augmentation in this patient can be barely appreciated post-HD in leads II and III.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ipej070085-03"/></fig><fig id="F4" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Augmentation in the amplitude of P-wave and QRS complex are evident in this patient post HD.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ipej070085-04"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Results from Re-measurement of P-wave parameters (N = 11)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ipej070085-05"/></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Baseline characteristics of patients (N = 17)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ipej070085-06"/></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T3" position="float"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Study variables before and after hemodialysis (N = 17)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ipej070085-07"/></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T4" position="float"><label>Table 4</label><caption><p>Echocardiographic parameters before and after hemodialysis (N = 17)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ipej070085-08"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>LA, left atrium; LV, left ventricle</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T5" position="float"><label>Table 5</label><caption><p>P duration before and after hemodialysis (N = 17)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ipej070085-09"/></table-wrap></floats-group></article>